    "q-97084": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97084",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}16{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}16{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =8[(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)-(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0-0)] \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{4-{4}^{-1}}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =15-16\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
