    "q-351897": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351897",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3/2}({cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{4x}{3})+7x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-2}}\n$)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{4x}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{4x}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\frac{8x}{3})+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}({cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{4x}{3})+7x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\frac{8x}{3})+1)+7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\frac{8x}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}+7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{16}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\frac{8x}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{3/2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{16}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\frac{8\\cdot (3/2)}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}]-[\\frac{3}{16}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4)+\\frac{69}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
