    "q-351899": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351899",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /10}({cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3})+2)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,$\n{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{5x}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\frac{10x}{3})+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /10}({cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3})+2)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /10}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\frac{10x}{3})+1)+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /10}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\frac{10x}{3})+\\frac{5}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{20}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\frac{10x}{3})+\\frac{5}{2}x)|}_{0}^{3\\pi /10} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{20}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\frac{10\\cdot (3\\pi /10)}{3})+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{10}]-[\\frac{3}{20}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
