    "q-352415": {
      "dom_id": "step-q352415",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }8{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)-\\,$\n{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }8{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4[(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\pi -\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0-0)] \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\pi )-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
